# rlwen.msg - Overseer Wen, dock office of the Shi (San Francisco, the Bilge)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

{100}{}{(A thin man in a Shi clerk's robe sits in the ruined waiting hall with an abacus on his knees.) Overseer Wen, dock office of the Shi. The Emperor tolerates what he controls. What he does not control, he does not tolerate for long. The Bilge may open. Fifteen percent of what it takes.}
{101}{}{Fifteen percent. Agreed.}
{102}{}{What if the Bilge were the Shi's eyes on the docks? Everyone who drinks here talks.}
{103}{}{[Barter] Ten. An open Bilge earns the Shi more than an empty one.}
{104}{}{Good evening, Overseer.}
{105}{}{Beads?}
{110}{}{(He moves three beads on the abacus.) Fifteen. The Shi will send someone every week. Don't make him wait.}
{111}{}{(For the first time Wen looks interested.) Every name, every boat, every cargo. A report each week. Ten percent, and when the Emperor is pleased, a Seal on your door. The tanker people must never know.}
{112}{}{(Wen studies you for a long time, then moves two beads.) Ten. You bargain like someone who has been to Chinatown before.}
{120}{}{(Wen doesn't look up.) You chose the tanker. The tanker floats; the Shi stay. When the tanker sinks, and it will, you'll need someone to stand on.}
{121}{}{Fifteen percent, then. Let's start over.}
{122}{}{(He moves three beads back.) Fifteen. The bosun will call it betrayal. The Shi call it good sense.}
{130}{}{(Wen nods.) The Bilge is tolerated. The Emperor's patience is long. It is not endless.}
{131}{}{Is there something the Shi need?}
{140}{}{A boat comes in at night with Hubologist money. It passes the docks without paying the Shi. Find out which boat, and there will be a Seal on your door.}
{142}{}{[Perception] I'll watch the boats.}
{143}{}{[Sneak] I'll get aboard and see for myself.}
{144}{}{Let's talk about something else.}
{145}{}{(Two nights later you give Wen a name and a hull number. He writes both down.) The Emperor is pleased. The Seal of the Shi can be fixed to your door.}

# The Bilge opens
{190}{}{An old woman has been sitting on the pier for days, waiting. Kwan. She ran the tea stall here when the ferries still ran. She knows everyone who ever waited in this hall.}
{191}{}{Send her in.}

# Look
{300}{}{A thin man in a clerk's robe with an abacus on his knees.}
{301}{}{Overseer Wen of the Shi dock office. He counts everything that floats.}
